WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
"Alright."

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:02.880
"Think Hard."

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:04.780
"What is it?"

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:06.920
"Circle."

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:08.880
"Close."

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:11.540
"But definitely wrong."

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.340
Let's say I showed you 2 shapes:

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.800
Shape 1, and shape 2.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.580
And then I ask you, which of these shapes matches the word "Bouba"

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:23.320
and which matches the word "Kiki"?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.860
For 98% of people, shape 1 is Kiki and shape 2 is Bouba.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.660
And it doesn't matter if you're from America, India, or if you're 2 and a half years old.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.080
This result is extremely consistent across all cultures and languages.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.100
So, what does it mean?

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.160
I want to suggest that with this study, we can see

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.020
how the brain gives abstract meaning

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.140
to many different shapes in a consistent way.

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.600
And filmmakers use this phenomenon to tell their story.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.980
The best place to see this idea has to be animation,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.100
where animators can make objects and characters from any shape they want.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.280
Let's start with the basics:

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.540
Circle, square, and triangle.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.580
This may seem reductive, but these shapes say a lot more than you may think.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.180
Evil characters are drawn like Kiki.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.100
They have sharper features;

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.300
pointy noses, ears, and long curly fingers.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.500
And the lovable characters are designed like Bouba;

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:02.420
soft and round.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.720
Is it a coincidence that the fluffiest, and least threatening character in animation is named "Baloo"?

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.980
To me, this whole concept makes intuitive sense.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.520
Our brains are designed to see sharp and pointy things as more threatening.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.280
"It is too round on the top, it needs to be pointy."

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.860
"Round is not scary."

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.260
"Pointy is scary."

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.380
Notice how I can draw the nicest and most recognizable character in animation with 3 circles.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:24.860
And notice how the devil is commonly represented as a collection of triangles.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.320
But do it a little more subtly,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.180
and you're able to emit an emotional response to the viewer without them even noticing.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.640
It happens during one of my favorite movie moments of all time,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.840
when we see the wicked villain transform from Kiki into a round, vulnerable Bouba.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.760
You may think this only applies to animation,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.660
but is it a coincidence that Darth Vader's mask has this shape,

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:54.140
and Lord Dark Helmet, the cartoonish and comedic parody of Darth Vader,

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:54.900
has this shape?

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.740
One of the sharpest villains is Maleficent,

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.360
and they even try to keep her pointy jawline and eyebrows in the live action remake.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.500
Following these same principles, what does it mean when you call somebody a "square"?

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.800
It means they're boring, maybe they're old fashioned.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:08.780
But a square can also symbolize something that is sturdy and firm.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.680
Sound familiar?

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.640
Squares are rigid and slow to change.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.520
If the world isn't how they want it to be, maybe they get a little angry.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.740
"Right after YOU eat THIS!"

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:17.380
"AAAUUGGGHHHH!!!"

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.280
If you really want to think abstractly,

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.080
the world of film exists inside a rectangular shape.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.440
A lot of important geometry comes from framing.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.880
The most significant framing to look for is probably the frame within the frame.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.820
This is used to show a character trapped in their current situation.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:36.620
*Various car horns*

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.160
Another less common, but equally important frame within a frame is the circle,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.040
which gives the geometric impression of surveillance and spying.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:50.560
*Booming silence*

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.840
To go a step deeper than the frame within the frame, you can use lines to create a visual jail cell in the frame.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:58.880
Whether it's through blinds in an office or even a computer screen,

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.960
a collection of vertical and horizontal lines can indicate a character is not only trapped,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.280
but held prisoner in their current lifestyle.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:11.500
These shots use geometry as a means of visual storytelling.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.420
"As far back as I can remember, I always wanted to be a gangster."

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.120
Without us even noticing it, framing is filled with lines.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.540
Geometry is everywhere, and the same principles apply.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:21.600
Triangles can emphasize power dynamics,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.720
linear framing can show physical separation,

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.680
or straight lines can guide our eyesight.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.200
So the next time you re watch your favorite movie, try analyzing the geometry of the framing and characters.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.880
If you do that, then you're in good shape.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.600
"What's this one?"

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.780
"It's a couple of wavy lines?..."

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:40.640
"Sorry."

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.180
"This isn't your lucky day!"

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.100
Thanks for watching.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:52.900
